{"name":"White Beet","hex":"#ebdfdd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"beet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Beta vulgaris, a plant with a swollen root which is eaten or used to make sugar.","A beetroot; a swollen root of such a plant used as a culinary vegetable."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To improve; to mend.","To kindle a fire."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Beetroot","blocks":[{"text":"Beetroot or beet is the taproot portion of a Beta vulgaris subsp. vulgaris plant in the Conditiva Group. The plant is a root vegetable also known as the table beet, garden beet, or dinner beet, or else categorized by color as red beet or golden beet. Its leaves constitute a leaf vegetable called beet greens. It is native to the Azores, Western Europe to the Mediterranean and India.\nBeetroot can be eaten raw, roasted, steamed, or boiled. Beetroot can also be canned, either whole or cut up, and often are pickled, spiced, or served in a sweet-and-sour sauce."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Detroitdarkredbeets.png/330px-Detroitdarkredbeets.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beetroot","wikidata":"Q165437"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Beets-Bundle.jpg/330px-Beets-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Beets-Bundle.jpg/960px-Beets-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Beets-Bundle.jpg/330px-Beets-Bundle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeets-Bundle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg/250px-Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg/500px-Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg/250px-Rote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARote_Bete_eine_Haelfte.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/A_basket_of_Beetroot.jpg/250px-A_basket_of_Beetroot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, htt